  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAnother prolonged period of dry weather begins today as long\nwave ridging begins to build in over the region atop a large\nanticyclone at the surface. The core of this high will \ngradually slide eastward from the Ohio Valley today through \nWednesday, becoming stalled in the Mid-Atlantic Region until \nFriday. In the meantime, temperatures across eastern New York \nand western New England will gradually moderate until above-\nnormal levels are reached Wednesday, persisting through the \nupcoming weekend. And while the passage of a weak, upper-level \nshortwave may act to increase cloud cover across some of our \nhigher terrain areas Tuesday, there won't be sufficient moisture\nto challenge this next dry stretch. \n\nAnother weak, upper-level shortwave associated with a weak\nsurface low passing by to our north looks to crest the ridge and swing\nthrough our region Friday. This additionally moisture-starved \nsystem will likely only act to increase cloud cover across \nareas within and north of the Capital Region as a back door cold\nfront sings through. Given very weak cold air advection \nresulting from this front and the immediate rebuild of the \nantecedent ridge and surface high, temperatures Saturday only \nlook to drop by a few degrees and will remain above normal.",
